{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And you shall not wrong, etc.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted (Bava Metzia 58b) that the verse speaks of verbal wrong (אוֹנָאַת דְּבָרִים). According to the plain sense of the verse, it further intends that although above He said אַל תּוֹנוּ and was precise to state immediately to inform that the verse speaks of wronging movable goods and not wronging land, the verse was concerned lest a person err and think that with land it is permitted to wrong; therefore He said לֹא תוֹנוּ, etc. And He said <b>and you shall fear your Elohim</b> — meaning: although there is no legal claim of אוֹנָאָה concerning them, that is specifically with regard to the jurisdiction of earthly courts; but there is always a prohibition of אוֹנָאָה in the matter. And the reason I said that there is no אוֹנָאָה in it is because I have set aside this judgment for Myself to judge it, and that is His saying <b>for I am YHWH your Elohim</b> — meaning, I judge you in this matter, not on the grounds that it is permitted to wrong in land. And we find many laws in the Torah where HaShem removed their judgment from an earthly court to the heavenly court; see what I wrote in Parashat Mishpatim (Exodus 21:12). And in our case there is a reason for the matter, because one may find a person selling something worth a hundred for fifty, or vice versa, and an earthly court cannot determine the matter to know which part of the transaction stems from genuine desire and which part stems from אוֹנָאָה; so HaShem removed its judgment to the One who examines hearts and knows a person's thoughts and schemes." "<b>And I have already</b> written above that a mistaken purchase exists even with land, and so the Rambam wrote in chapter 15 of Hilkhot Mekhirah: 'And likewise one who sells land to his fellow, etc., and a defect is found in the purchase that the buyer did not know about — he may return it even after several years,' end quote. And our explanation is what the Sages, may their memory be blessed, encompassed in their statement when they said: אַל תּוֹנוּ — in verbal wrong (אוֹנַת דְּבָרִים), for in this every kind of אוֹנָאָה is included, since also one who wrongs his fellow to deceive him with false words is considered מְאַנֵּהוּ (wronging him), and behold he falls under the category of אוֹנַת דְּבָרִים."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:01:12.493801127Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}